"executive_summary": "This paper addresses the scalability and modeling challenges of probabilistic time series forecasting by proposing a novel framework that blends the computational efficiency of recurrent neural networks (RNNs) with the high-quality probabilistic modeling of diffusion models. Traditional autoregressive models, such as RNNs and Transformers, are widely used but often rely on simple parametric output layers (e.g., Gaussian), limiting their ability to capture complex, high-dimensional distributions and cross-feature dependencies. While generative models like Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPM) and Flow Matching (FM) offer superior distributional modeling, they face scalability issues when applied to long time series windows due to the model size being proportional to both feature dimension and window length. The authors introduce a method based on Stochastic Interpolants (SI), extended to a conditional generation framework with additional control features. The core innovation is the use of an RNN to encode the historical context into a history prompt, which then conditions the SI module to predict the next time step. This approach avoids mapping from pure noise to data, instead mapping from the current time point to the next, leveraging the strong correlations between neighboring time points. Theoretical contributions include extending SI theory to arbitrary joint distributions and conditional generation. Empirically, the method is evaluated on synthetic datasets and four real-world multivariate time series datasets (Solar, Exchange, Traffic, Wiki). The proposed SI-based model outperforms or matches baselines (DDPM, SGM, FM, Vec-LSTM) on three out of four datasets in terms of CRPS-sum, demonstrating superior uncertainty quantification and predictive accuracy. The framework offers a computational-friendly alternative for large-scale generative time series prediction.",
